 The metrics are kept deliberately distinct — longitudinal control for the braking point, a target value for corner speed, lateral path accuracy for the apex — so the three elements can in principle fade on independent trajectories rather than moving together.
 
-Tolerance bands are not the raw variance of the reference laps. An AI driver has no biological motor noise, while even expert human performance never eliminates variability — inherent motor noise in the human nervous system persists at all skill levels (Cohen & Sternad, 2009) — so bands derived directly from the AI's lap-to-lap spread would be impossibly narrow for any human. Each band is therefore **floored and source-dependent**: `band_m = max(σ_ref,m × EVM_source, floor_m)`. The multiplier — the **Expertise Variance Multiplier (EVM)** — compensates for the reference's missing motor noise, with a provisional default of **EVM_AI = 3.0** for AI-sourced corners, calibrated empirically in the pilot by comparing real human variance against the AI baseline. Two refinements guard the structure itself. First, the **absolute per-metric floor** exists because the AI is consistent by construction: at some corners σ_ref may be near zero, and any multiplier of nearly-nothing is still nearly-nothing — without a floor the band is hostage to an arbitrarily small baseline. Second, corners whose reference is **human-substituted** (see Method) already contain human motor variability in σ_ref; applying the AI multiplier there would count motor noise twice, so those corners take a smaller multiplier, **EVM_human** (provisional default 1.25), calibrated alongside EVM_AI. Naming the parameters rather than burying them follows the precedent of Bugeja et al. (2017), whose Assetto Corsa feedback system built explicit `tolerance` parameters into its detection algorithms (e.g., slip-ratio and racing-line tolerances) and set them by empirical observation.
+Tolerance bands are not the raw variance of the reference laps. An AI driver has no biological motor noise, while even expert human performance never eliminates variability — inherent motor noise in the human nervous system persists at all skill levels (Cohen & Sternad, 2009) — so bands derived directly from the AI's lap-to-lap spread would be impossibly narrow for any human. Each band is therefore **floored and source-dependent**: `band_m = max(σ_ref,m × EVM_source, floor_m)`. The multiplier — the **Expertise Variance Multiplier (EVM)** — compensates for the reference's missing motor noise, with a provisional default of **EVM_AI = 3.0** for AI-sourced corners, calibrated empirically in the pilot by comparing real human variance against the AI baseline. Two refinements guard the structure itself. First, the **absolute per-metric floor** exists because the AI is consistent by construction: at some corners σ_ref may be near zero, and any multiplier of nearly-nothing is still nearly-nothing — without a floor the band is hostage to an arbitrarily small baseline. Second, corners whose reference is **human-substituted** (see Method) already contain human motor variability in σ_ref; applying the AI multiplier there would count motor noise twice, so those corners take a smaller multiplier, **EVM_human** (provisional default 1.25), calibrated alongside EVM_AI. Naming the parameters rather than burying them is a transparency choice of this design, not a borrowed one. **[CORRECTED 2026-10-14 — this sentence previously cited Bugeja et al. (2017) as the precedent for empirically set `tolerance` parameters, including slip-ratio and racing-line tolerances. No accessible version of that paper supports the claim: the abstract and repository record describe a heuristic rule system, and no tolerance value or threshold-setting method is visible. The citation is withdrawn here and kept only in the §42/§48 sense, that its gains did not survive feedback removal. See `BAND_CALIBRATION_BRIEF.md`, "The answer, 2026-10-14".]** The band-setting method that replaces it is standard setting: no published rule exists for a bandwidth width (the paradigm's own reviews say so), so the floors are placed between early-practice and plateau performance by the contrasting-groups method (Jørgensen, Konge & Subhi, 2018) and the decision window is sized by minimal detectable change (Weir, 2005). **A second correction to this paragraph:** with the AI's measured σ_ref of ~0.02 m, `σ_ref × EVM_AI` ≈ 0.06 m never reaches any floor, so every band in every reported run was its floor and the EVM decided nothing. It must be presented as a specified-but-inoperative parameter; if a human reference is ever recorded, Gallagher et al. (2004)'s criterion at 2 SD from the expert mean is the citable form of the same idea.
 
